Example data sources and reuse status

The MIT license covers the package code. It does not establish permission to
redistribute data copied from a publication. Publication is blocked until the
maintainer records a confirmed reuse basis for the wheat data below.

piepho2004_wheat_pairs.csv / piepho2004_wheat
Source: Piepho (2004), An Algorithm for a Letter-Based Representation of All-
Pairwise Comparisons, doi:10.1198/1061860043515; reproduced in Table 7 of
Ennis, Fayle, and Ennis (2012), Assignment-Minimum Clique Coverings,
doi:10.1145/2133803.2275596.
Content: significant/non-significant decisions for the 190 unordered pairs of
20 wheat treatments. The repository stores these decisions as CSV rows.
R data-raw/datasets.R reads labels as text and decisions as logical values,
then saves the same rows as piepho2004_wheat.rda. Python examples copy the CSV.
Reuse permission: unconfirmed. A citation alone does not grant permission.
Owner of follow-up: John Ennis, proposed package maintainer.

simple_abc_to_ac_pairs.csv / simple_abc_pairs
simple_abc_to_ac_means.csv / simple_abc_means
Source: the five-group illustrative example in this repository. Its relationship
graph and means reproduce the ABC-to-AC teaching example. No independent source
or specific data license is recorded in the repository.
Content: ten pairwise decisions and five numeric means. R data-raw/datasets.R
reads the CSV files and saves the corresponding data frames. Python examples
copy the CSV files.
Reuse basis and origin: maintainer confirmation required before publication.
Owner of follow-up: John Ennis.

Do not interpret this notice as a new license or as confirmed permission.
Retain it in every distribution that contains the example data.
